<title>MEXICO: List of firms named in Mexico HFCS dumping case.</title>
<headline>List of firms named in Mexico HFCS dumping case.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-02-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico's government named 17 companies involved in the import dumping investigation of high fructose corn syrup (HFCS) on the Mexican market. A full list of the companies follows.</p>
<p>The Mexican Sugar Chamber alleges disloyal trade practices against the following companies:</p>
<p>-- Almidones Mexicanos SA</p>
<p>-- Cargill de Mexico SA, a unit of Cargill Inc.</p>
<p>-- Arancia SA</p>
<p>-- Arancia CPC SA, a JV between Arancia and CPC Intl</p>
<p>-- Embotelladora Mizarlo SA</p>
<p>-- Grupo Tropicana del Occidente SA</p>
<p>-- Productos de Maiz SA</p>
<p>-- Administracion Sim SA</p>
<p>-- Cadbury y Bebidas SA, unit of Cadbury Schweppes</p>
<p>-- Dulces Arbor SA</p>
<p>The Mexican Sugar Chamber alleges imports at &quot;prices below normal value&quot; against the following companies:</p>
<p>-- A.E. Staley Mfg Company, owned by Tate &amp; Lyle</p>
<p>-- Archer Daniels Midland Company Corn Processing</p>
<p>--  American Fructose Corp.</p>
<p>-- American Maize Products Co., owned by Cerestar USA Inc.</p>
<p>--  Cargill Inc.</p>
<p>-- CPC International Inc.</p>
<p>--  Hubinger Roquette America</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7289530))</p>
